## 2.x Model development and validation

The ${data_noun_en} (`${data_file}`) were analysed with a machine-learning pipeline designed to
avoid information leakage between training and test data. The dataset comprised
${n_rows} measurements from ${n_groups} independent samples (${reps_text_en}); ${batch_text_en}.
Each ${record_noun_en} covered ${range_text} and was treated as a ${n_features}-dimensional input vector.

**Preprocessing.** Preprocessing chains (${prep_text}) were treated as a hyperparameter
rather than being fixed a priori, so that the choice of preprocessing was itself subject to
internal cross-validation. All transformations that estimate parameters from data — multiplicative
scatter correction reference spectra, feature standardisation — were fitted **only on the training
fold** and applied unchanged to the held-out fold.

**Validation protocol.** Model performance was estimated by nested cross-validation with grouping
by sample identifier (`${group_col}`), so that repeated measurements of the same specimen never
appeared in both training and test sets. The outer loop used ${k_out}-fold grouped
cross-validation repeated ${n_repeats} times with different random partitions; the inner loop used
${k_in}-fold grouped cross-validation for hyperparameter selection. Hyperparameters were never
selected using the outer test folds. Reported metrics are the mean over repetitions with the
standard deviation across repetitions, i.e. the fold means were first averaged within each
repetition and the between-repetition variability was then summarised.

**Models.** Candidate models were ${models_text}. Partial least squares (PLS) regression was
retained as a mandatory baseline given its status in chemometrics, and variable importance in
projection (VIP) scores were reported for the PLS solution.

**Uncertainty and comparison.** 95% confidence intervals were obtained by cluster (sample-level)
bootstrap, resampling specimens rather than individual rows to respect within-sample correlation.
Differences between models were assessed on paired out-of-fold predictions with a paired bootstrap
test; the resulting p-values were corrected for multiplicity using the Benjamini–Hochberg
procedure. ${ci_text_en}

**Overfitting control.** A Y-randomization test was performed by permuting the response at the
sample level (preserving the grouping structure and the fold definitions) and re-running the
outer modelling procedure ${perm_mode_note_en} ${perm_text_en}. ${perm_conclusion_en}

